 "drivingQuestion": "What is the abuvro'e of the mishna?",
 "outcome": "not_dialectical",
 "outcomeNote": "Definitional and medical glosses on the mishna",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.109b.8",
   "daf": "109b",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "וְשׁוֹתִין אַבּוּבְרוֹאֶה. מַאי אַבּוּבְרוֹאֶה? חוּמַטְרִיָּא. מַאי חוּמַטְרִיָּא? חוּטְרָא יְחִידָאָה. לְמַאי עָבְדִי לַהּ — לְגִילּוּיָא. וְאִי לָא — לַיְתֵי חַמְשָׁא כְּלִילֵי וְחַמְשָׁא כּוֹסָתָא דְשִׁיכְרָא, וְנִישְׁלוֹקִינְהוּ בַּהֲדֵי הֲדָדֵי עַד דְּקַיְימָא אַאַנְפָּקָא, וְנִישְׁתֵּי.",
   "en": "We learned the mishna: «And one may drink abuvro’e» on Shabbat. The Gemara asks: «What is abuvro’e?» The Gemara answers: It is the plant known as «ḥumtareya.» The Gemara asks: «What is ḥumtareya?» It is that which is called «the lonely staff.» The Gemara asks: «For what» purpose «is it used?» It is used «for» one who drank «exposed» water from which we suspect a snake drank and left behind its venom. «And if» one does «not» have the lonely staff, or if it was ineffective, «let» him «take five roses and five cups of beer and cook them together until there is a quarter» of a log of liquid «and» then «drink» it."
  }
 ],
